    "lawCode": "Amd §§4002 & 4008, Pub Health L",
    "lawSection": "Public Health Law",
    "memo": "BILL NUMBER:S5554\n\nTITLE OF BILL:\nAn act\nto amend the public health law, in relation to permitting a hospice to\nemploy and enter into contracts with licensed healthcare providers\n\nPURPOSE:\nThe bill extends the scope of hospice care to people with a life\nexpectancy of twelve months from the current six, and would permit a\nhospice to employ or contract with licensed health care professionals\nto provide health care services that the hospice is authorized to\nprovide to its patients.\n\nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\nThe bill amends section 4002 of the Public Health Law to define\n\"terminally ill\" to mean that a patient has a medical prognosis that\nthe individual's life expectancy is approximately one year or less if\nthe illness runs its normal course. Current state law does not define\nthe term, but Medicare uses a definition of six months. The bill\namends section 4008 of the Public Health Law to permit hospices to\ndirectly employ or contract with licensed health care professionals\nto provide health care services that the hospice is authorized to\nprovide to its patients.\n\nJUSTIFICATION:\nStudies by Kaiser Permanente, Aetna, and others have found that\npalliative care services improve patient satisfaction while reducing\nhealth care costs.\nOver time, the experience of palliative end-of-life care-giving has\nlengthened, partially due to medical innovations as well as an\nimproved understanding of the death process. Several states have\nmodified their laws to enable hospice care for up to one year. The\nMedicaid Redesign Team proposed a similar change for New York in MRT\nproposal 209.\n\nThis definition would not change what Medicare pays for, but it would\nallow a hospice to serve patients for payment other than by Medicare.\n\nHospices are authorized to contract with health care providers to\nprovide health care services to the hospice's patients, provided that\nthe hospice maintains full responsibility for the planning,\ncoordination and quality of such services and the adherence to the\nplan of care established for the patients. However, there is concern\nthat the rules against corporate practice of licensed professions bar\na hospice from directly employing or contracting with licensed\nprofessionals.\n\nCertain entities, such as hospitals under PHL Art. 28, may do so. This\nlegislation would enable hospices to do the same. This would simplify\nand improve their operation.\n\nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\nNew bill.\n\nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\nMedicaid savings.\n\nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nImmediately.\n\n",
